# Assessing Driver Response to Blue Flashing Lights for Speed Management in Freeway Work Zones: A Driving Simulator Study in Mexico

**Juan Daniel Olivares-Herrera¹, Antonio Hurtado-Beltran²*, Nelio Pastor-Gómez³ and Rafael Soto-Espitia⁴**

¹ School of Civil Engineering, Universidad Michoacana de San Nicolás de Hidalgo; juan.olivares@umich.mx  
² School of Civil Engineering, Universidad Michoacana de San Nicolás de Hidalgo; antonio.hurtado@umich.mx  
³ School of Civil Engineering, Universidad Michoacana de San Nicolás de Hidalgo; npastor@umich.mx  
⁴ School of Civil Engineering, Universidad Michoacana de San Nicolás de Hidalgo; rsoto@umich.mx  

\* Correspondence: antonio.hurtado@umich.mx

---

## Abstract

Effective speed management in freeway work zones is essential for improving driver safety, particularly where temporary traffic control requires substantial speed reductions. Conventional yellow flashing lights are widely used to enhance work zone visibility; however, their effectiveness may diminish due to driver habituation. Blue flashing lights have emerged as a promising alternative, although their influence on driver behavior remains insufficiently understood.

This study evaluates driver responses to blue flashing lights using a first-person driving simulator integrating the traffic microsimulation platform SUMO with the 3D visualization capabilities of Unity. Two experimental scenarios were evaluated: (1) conventional yellow flashing lights and (2) experimental blue flashing lights, both mounted on temporary traffic control devices within the transition area. The simulated environment replicates a work zone on the Maravatío–Zapotlanejo (MEX-015D) toll freeway in Mexico, where the speed limit decreases from 110 to 60 km/h. Fifty-one licensed drivers experienced both scenarios under controlled geometric, operational, and environmental conditions.

Behavioral measures, including speed profiles, acceleration, deceleration, and lane-changing maneuvers, were recorded and analyzed. Results indicate that blue flashing lights produced consistently lower operating speeds than conventional yellow lights, with the greatest effects observed in the advance warning and transition areas, where speed reductions reached 9.3 and 8.3 km/h, respectively.

This study provides the first simulator-based evaluation of blue flashing lights in multilane freeway work zones in Mexico, offering controlled experimental evidence supporting their potential application as a perceptual speed management treatment in developing countries.

## Keywords

Work zone; blue light; driving behavior; driving simulator; freeway; speed.

